---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// "Data shaping" — the guide for turning raw input into the exact view-model a
// logic-less FlatBars template renders. JSONata is the tool; this is NOT a fifth
// surface and NOT a JSONata language reference (the long tail links to
// jsonata.org). Every runnable cell + the flagship round-trip come from the one
// gate-validated source (../jsonata.mjs, gated by scripts/check-jsonata.mjs), so
// a snippet can never drift from what the Lab's vendored engine produces.
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import TryJsonata from "../components/TryJsonata.jsx";
import { sections, flagship, taughtFunctions } from "../jsonata.mjs";

const navLabels = {
  basics: "The basics", strings: "Strings", numbers: "Numbers & aggregation",
  arrays: "Arrays", objects: "Objects", booleans: "Booleans & tests",
  datetime: "Date & time", regex: "Regular expressions", "higher-order": "Higher-order",
};
const groups = [
  {
    label: "Data shaping",
    items: [
      { href: "#intro", label: "Why shape data" },
      ...sections.map((s) => ({ href: `#${s.id}`, label: navLabels[s.id] || s.title })),
      { href: "#flagship", label: "Flagship: a changelog" },
      { href: "#beyond", label: "Beyond this guide" },
    ],
  },
];
const fnCount = taughtFunctions.length;
---

<Reference
  title="Data shaping"
  subtitle="FlatBars templates are logic-less on purpose. This runnable guide shows where the logic goes: JSONata reshapes raw input into the exact view-model your template renders — every snippet runs live through the engine the Lab ships."
  groups={groups}
  dialect="maxbars"
>
  <style>
    .ds-cell { margin: 1rem 0 1.6rem; }
    .ds-cell .ds-h { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem; }
    .ds-cell .ds-h b { color: var(--accent-2); font-family: var(--font-mono); }
    .ds-cell .ds-h span { color: var(--fg-muted); }
    .ds-flow { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
      font: 600 12px/1.4 var(--font-mono); margin: 1.1rem 0; }
    .ds-flow .box { padding: 0.35rem 0.6rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
      border-radius: 6px; background: var(--bg-1); color: var(--fg-secondary); }
    .ds-flow .box.mid { color: var(--accent-2); border-color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-softer); }
    .ds-flow .arr { color: var(--fg-faint); }
  </style>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Intro ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="intro">
    <h2>Why shape data</h2>
    <p>
      Say your data is a flat list of git commits and you want a grouped changelog —
      Features, then Fixes, then Chores, each counted, empty groups dropped. Where
      does that grouping happen? Not in the template: every FlatBars surface is
      <b>logic-less</b> by design (even MaxBars keeps its sugar thin), so a template
      says <em>what</em> the output looks like, never computes <em>which</em> data to
      show. The computing happens <b>before</b> rendering — you reshape the raw input
      into a clean <b>view-model</b>, an object shaped exactly like the template
      expects, and the template just walks it. (That changelog is the
      <a href="#flagship">worked example</a> at the end.)
    </p>
    <div class="ds-flow" aria-label="data to view to output">
      <span class="box">raw data</span><span class="arr">→</span>
      <span class="box mid">JSONata transform</span><span class="arr">→</span>
      <span class="box">view-model</span><span class="arr">→</span>
      <span class="box">template → output</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      The tool for that middle step is <a href="https://jsonata.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JSONata ↗</a>,
      a small query-and-transform language for JSON. The Lab ships it as the
      <b>transform</b> tab, and so does this guide: the cells below run the exact
      same engine. Edit either pane — the result re-evaluates as you type.
    </p>
    <TryJsonata client:visible data={sections[0].cells[0].data} expr={sections[0].cells[0].expr} />
    <p class="tip">
      This guide teaches the <b>{fnCount}</b> JSONata functions FlatBars users reach
      for most, each demonstrated live. The full function library lives at
      <a href="https://docs.jsonata.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs.jsonata.org ↗</a> — we
      don’t fork it here.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.map((sec) => (
    <section id={sec.id}>
      <h2>{sec.title}</h2>
      <p>{sec.lead}</p>
      {sec.functions.length > 0 && (
        <p class="tip">Functions: {sec.functions.map((f, i) => <span>{i > 0 ? " · " : ""}<code>{f}</code></span>)}</p>
      )}
      {sec.cells.map((c, i) => (
        <div class="ds-cell">
          <p class="ds-h"><b>{c.label}</b> — <span>{c.note}</span></p>
          {/* The first basics cell already ran in the intro; show the rest. */}
          {!(sec.id === "basics" && i === 0) && <TryJsonata client:visible data={c.data} expr={c.expr} />}
        </div>
      ))}
    </section>
  ))}

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Flagship ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="flagship">
    <h2>Flagship: a changelog, end to end</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the whole idea in one cell. The <b>data</b> is a flat list of
      conventional commits. The <b>transform</b> groups them by type, fixes the
      section order, drops empty groups, and labels each — producing a view-model
      of titled sections. The <b>MaxBars</b> template has <em>zero</em> branching:
      it just walks <code>sections</code> and <code>items</code>. All the logic is
      upstream, as data.
    </p>
    <OpenInLab
      client:visible
      engine={flagship.engine}
      data={flagship.data}
      transform={flagship.transform}
      template={flagship.template}
    />
    <p class="tip">
      Notice <code>$$.commits</code> in the transform: inside a mapping the context
      is the current section key, so the root reference <code>$$</code> reaches back
      to the original input. <b>Open in Lab</b> carries the data, transform, and
      template across together.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── Beyond ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="beyond">
    <h2>Beyond this guide</h2>
    <p>
      This is the data-shaping subset, not all of JSONata. For the complete
      function library, operators, and semantics — date/time pictures, the full
      regex surface, <code>$reduce</code> patterns, partial application — go to the
      source:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://docs.jsonata.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs.jsonata.org ↗</a> — the language reference.</li>
      <li><a href="https://try.jsonata.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">try.jsonata.org ↗</a> — the official Exerciser, a full playground.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Back in FlatBars, every surface pairs with this step:
      <a href="/minbars">MinBars</a>, <a href="/classicbars">ClassicBars</a>, and
      <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> all render a view-model you shaped here.
    </p>
  </section>
</Reference>
